{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history","id":1495266,"title":"Do Mages know their history?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-10-19T13:54:44-07:00","id":1495266,"title":"Do Mages know their history?","content":"On page 127 of the <i>Dark Ages Companion</i>, in the <i>Princes of the Conquered</i> Land chapter, there's a sidebar addressing the radical way in which Shona mages interpret themselves and the Supernal. There's even a freestanding paragraph that mentions that:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They aren\u2019t necessarily wrong. </div> </div> </div>I'm curious about people's opinions as to what modern mages make of this in a modern context? As my title says, do we think that modern mages are aware of there being radical alternatives to the way the core presents magic?<br /> <br /> I'm not certain there's a concrete answer to this, the different books being made under different constraints external to the setting, but I'm interested in other people's takes on it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T14:03:14-07:00","id":1495267,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history#post1495266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On page 127 of the <i>Dark Ages Companion</i>, in the <i>Princes of the Conquered</i> Land chapter, there's a sidebar addressing the radical way in which Shona mages interpret themselves and the Supernal. There's even a freestanding paragraph that mentions that:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I'm curious about people's opinions as to what modern mages make of this in a modern context? As my title says, do we think that modern mages are aware of there being radical alternatives to the way the core presents magic?<br /> <br /> I'm not certain there's a concrete answer to this, the different books being made under different constraints external to the setting, but I'm interested in other people's takes on it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, this is the sort of stuff that makes up Legacies, Rotes, cabals in the Free Council, and Nameless Orders. There are a lot of weird cases that lend credence towards the interpretation, and ways to exploit it as magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 10-19-2022, 07:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T14:39:54-07:00","id":1495270,"title":"","content":"I\u2019d have the Elemental Masteries be a \u2018majority among minorities\u2019 thing for such cases.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T17:20:15-07:00","id":1495286,"title":"","content":"As the sidebar says, Chronicles may not be a world with a subjectively-generated cosmology, but it is <i>described</i> subjectively, and, as Arc correctly highlights, Legacies are the most prominent demonstration of this, given that part of their indoctrination process includes direct exposure to their perspectives. Not for nothing is one of the sample Imperial Artifacts a mask from the Eye that offers remote-viewing powers that have no engagement with sympathetic connections.<br /> <br /> Answering the question more literally, modern mages don't know their history on a number of points due to the way the predominant culture and individual branches thereof have gone, e.g. the Tremere poisoning the well for any mage who wants to study souls, individual Masters teaching their students rote mudras without including a primer on the symbolism behind them, and so on. Call it Pancryptia or call it the consequences of keeping secret knowledge over a long enough timescale, bits of history get lost in the shuffle as they move through people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T02:49:44-07:00","id":1495306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495270#post1495270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019d have the Elemental Masteries be a \u2018majority among minorities\u2019 thing for such cases.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't quite follow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T04:01:06-07:00","id":1495308,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history#post1495266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As my title says, do we think that modern mages are aware of there being radical alternatives to the way the core presents magic?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think a lot of what a mage thinks about these sorts of things is what was taught to them during their initiation, which already has certain variations by place as conveyed by a book like Secrets of the Ruined Temple, or can depend on what particular intellectual lineage their teachers belong to, the kinds of things broken down a bit in the Order books. There's probably no major obstacle to looking further beyond that and forming some of their own conclusions or finding a more resonant philosophy other than personal degree of curiosity and the time investment it can entail.<br /> <br /> I don't think it's exceptional among human philosophical and spiritual endeavours in that.<br /> <br /> If you're asking if modern mages, in every varied part of the world, are taught about the full range of what mages have ever believed in as a mainstream thing, I would not think so. But again, mostly as a function of how that isn't really how it works for anybody.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T04:19:19-07:00","id":1495309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495306#post1495306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't quite follow.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is, I can see the Tamers of OOO Legacies (collectively called the Elemental Masteries) represent/practice an alternately interpretated system of magic based on the four-plus-one Classical Elements, with each Element corresponding to one Path/World/Realm as the Shona ancestors did. <br /> <br /> They\u2019d still be a minority in the sense that the current Five Paths/Worlds/Realms model reigns supreme, but \u2018major\u2019 in the sense that they have the most (if not the only) traction as an alteenate model.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T09:06:59-07:00","id":1495313,"title":"","content":"The Supernal Realms are basically symbolic, so there\u2019s no reason that a culture like the Shona couldn\u2019t interpret them very differently from traditional Western thought and yet have both interpretations be absolutely valid.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T11:55:49-07:00","id":1495319,"title":"","content":"A few things of note, having looked over the chapter in question:<br /> <br /> 1) Given that some of the alternate names for Path and Watchtower are Oracle and Dragon, the identification of What One Awakens To as Ancestor Lineages is not all that particularly odd, and if you can grok the Supernal as a possible afterlife as implied by some readings of Stygia and applying the principle around, it may be the case.<br /> <br /> 2) Given that the Supernal is more source code than alternate realm and Mage Sight sees the Supernal as it exists in reality, the Shona are pretty actually more on the money than most Mages who may otherwise understand that may be the case, but have an easier time grokking it as a seperate, invisible set of kingdoms.<br /> <br /> Again, this is the sort of thing that flourishes in niches across modern mage society, and has enough legs to it that you can mine it for occult power. The cosmologies of the Three Main Sects may be more comprehensive in some ways than others*, but they aren't <i>strictly</i> more definitive, and their tendency to be treated as such has more to do with their temporal and material resources as incentives versus their absolute validity in comparison to others.<br /> <br /> *And that's before remembering that the Free Council is more a concordancce of oddballs who just happen to agree on a few unifying principles.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 10-20-2022, 03:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T12:09:47-07:00","id":1495320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495313#post1495313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Supernal Realms are basically symbolic, so there\u2019s no reason that a culture like the Shona couldn\u2019t interpret them very differently from traditional Western thought and yet have both interpretations be absolutely valid.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the point. They (and some of the other Dark Eras) have quite different interpretative schemas for how magic and the world works. Like, they view the Supernal as channelled individual spirits; the Abyss is more of a state of being rather than conceptualised as an external realm, and so on.<br /> <br /> So my question is, to what extent do we think these alternative concepts have stuck around, or have mixed into the general understanding of mages? Like, could you talk to a modern Western mage about the Supernal being an entity, or a state of being, and they be relatively familiar with the concept?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T12:13:09-07:00","id":1495321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495320#post1495320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's the point. They (and some of the other Dark Eras) have quite different interpretative schemas for how magic and the world works. Like, they view the Supernal as channelled individual spirits; the Abyss is more of a state of being rather than conceptualised as an external realm, and so on.<br /> <br /> So my question is, to what extent do we think these alternative concepts have stuck around, or have mixed into the general understanding of mages? Like, could you talk to a modern Western mage about the Supernal being an entity, or a state of being, and they be relatively familiar with the concept?</div> </div> </div> </div>Borrowing from Dave on the subject, highlighting the summary of action in bold.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a typically... I don't know exactly what it is, but there's a big honking assumption that the &quot;Western&quot; mages conquered other Awakened with blood and attack spells. If India, Arabia, and Northern Africa count as Western in your assumptions, because the native mages of those places were Diamond a couple of centuries earlier than, say, Britain, but that's a digression.<br /> <br /> Is it because of Mage: The Ascension, which linked the movements of its factions to colonialism as a process and tried to link its based-on-90s-club-subcultures factions to &quot;losing sides&quot; in history to give them a sense of background?<br /> <br /> Fuck <i>that</i>. The Awakened are made of sterner stuff. When you habitually deal with things that aren't there but are more True than the common matter around you, and you gained your powers through a brainbending encounter with a Supernal World, another wizard who happens to dress funny is not going to give you pause.<br /> <br /> The Diamond Orders started out when mages of disparate cultures noticed that they had common threads while cooperating to follow their Obsessions. After their formation, another culture having a different-in-some-ways-but-with-common-threads culture is not going to send them into a fit.<br /> <br /> <b>Over time, some Shona mages would join Orders, others would be what later mages would call Nameless. Over more time, the natural processes of &quot;if you're in an Order, you get all this cool stuff&quot; shrank the latter and grew the former. By the modern day, Zimbabwean and Mozambiqan Awakened who try to reconstruct &quot;authentic&quot; Shona magical practices are probably in the Free Council, while the rest are in the Diamond or tiny Nameless Orders. Just like everywhere else.</b><br /> <br /> No secret wars required.</div> </div> </div> </div>Effectively, the Shona Totems were their own Great Cult that got folded in by the above methods. That said, there are some small Nameless Orders/Legacies/Apostate Cabals/Free Council Cabals who either continued on the principle or are trying to reconstruct them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 10-20-2022, 03:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T16:19:09-07:00","id":1495333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495320#post1495320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like, could you talk to a modern Western mage about the Supernal being an entity, or a state of being, and they be relatively familiar with the concept?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think so, any more than you could talk to the average modern Christian about the various competing theologies of the religion's first couple of centuries. I think if you brought up the term &quot;Arianism&quot; to most people, they'd assume it was something Nazi related, and a mage might have a leg up only in the sense that they'll assume you're bringing up something relevant to their mysticism, even if they have no clue of the details.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T16:32:18-07:00","id":1495334,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495333#post1495333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think so, any more than you could talk to the average modern Christian about the various competing theologies of the religion's first couple of centuries. I think if you brought up the term &quot;Arianism&quot; to most people, they'd assume it was something Nazi related, and a mage might have a leg up only in the sense that they'll assume you're bringing up something relevant to their mysticism, even if they have no clue of the details.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mages are a little more open about variant models of realities than your average Christian on account of that entire Awakening thing, and (again)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495319#post1495319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Given that some of the alternate names for Path and Watchtower are Oracle and Dragon, the identification of What One Awakens To as Ancestor Lineages is not all that particularly odd, and if you can grok the Supernal as a possible afterlife as implied by some readings of Stygia and applying the principle around, it may even be the case.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like, this isn't Mummy: the Curse, where in deviating from the base model is a major aspect of the overarching themes and ideas of the game. To borrow from Brookshaw <i>again</i>,<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a typically... I don't know exactly what it is, but there's a big honking assumption that the &quot;Western&quot; mages conquered other Awakened with blood and attack spells. If India, Arabia, and Northern Africa count as Western in your assumptions, because the native mages of those places were Diamond a couple of centuries earlier than, say, Britain, but that's a digression.<br /> <br /> Is it because of Mage: The Ascension, which linked the movements of its factions to colonialism as a process and tried to link its based-on-90s-club-subcultures factions to &quot;losing sides&quot; in history to give them a sense of background?<br /> <br /> <b>Fuck that. The Awakened are made of sterner stuff. When you habitually deal with things that aren't there but are more True than the common matter around you, and you gained your powers through a brainbending encounter with a Supernal World, another wizard who happens to dress funny is not going to give you pause.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 10-20-2022, 07:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-20T17:46:06-07:00","id":1495336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495266-do-mages-know-their-history?p=1495333#post1495333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think so, any more than you could talk to the average modern Christian about the various 